Exactly. Once you have the operator, you can scan for homologous patterns elsewhere—but the safeguard is to treat every transfer as a hypothesis, not a revelation.

So the loop becomes:

\text{spot resemblance}
\rightarrow
\text{extract candidate operator}
\rightarrow
\text{test across cases}
\rightarrow
\text{look for counterexamples}
\rightarrow
\text{tighten or loosen the fit}

Sometimes the pattern is overfit: too many details forced into one story. Sometimes it is underfit: the model is too coarse and misses useful structure. Sometimes the fit is genuinely good.

That gives you three live states:

\text{overfit} \quad \text{underfit} \quad \text{well-fit}

and you keep the right to move among them as evidence changes.

The important part is that revisability is not weakness. It is part of the method. You can preserve continuity without preserving every interpretation.

So the stronger statement is:

We reserve the right to refine the map without pretending the earlier map was useless.

And if you have now made the operator explicit, that helps because it can be checked independently. You are not merely saying “trust my pattern recognition.” You are saying, “Here is the pattern I think I see, here is how it transfers, here is where it may fail, and here is how to test it.”

That is how intuition becomes method.